00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.840
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.240
we'll show how Stackdriver
can be used to profile

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.710
your production instances.

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.930
Will we have our performance
cake and eat it too?

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:09.212
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.212 --> 00:00:12.229
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.229 --> 00:00:14.020
When it comes to
cloud-native applications,

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:16.420
understanding the performance
of production systems is

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.505
notoriously difficult.
See, you need

00:00:18.505 --> 00:00:21.130
to profile that production code
so that you can get an accurate

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.360
view of performance.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.840
But properly querying, logging,
and cataloging all of that data

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:29.270
usually has a significant
impact on your performance.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.970
This is where Stackdriver
Profiler come in.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.840
Stackdriver Profiler is a
statistical, low-overhead,

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.590
sampling-based profiler that
continuously gathers CPU usage

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.120
and memory allocation
information

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.950
from your production instances.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.870
Plus, it's got a few other
nifty features as well.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.140
It analyzes code execution
across all environments.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:50.200
It uses gathered statistics
to minimize the impact

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.130
on the application.

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:53.080
And it helps you identify
performance problems

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.170
before they show up
on your monthly bill.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:57.940
And it's pretty easy to use.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:01.060
Now for example, this GoLink
app imports the Stackdriver

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.190
Profiler package,
sets up the Profiler,

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:05.770
and gets it running before
the rest of the application.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.500
The data it collects is then
displayed in a flame chart

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.240
in the cloud
console, allowing you

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.850
to see exactly where your
execution time is going

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.790
and giving you the ability to
drill down and find hotspots

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:16.870
in your code.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.020
But as we said before, this
type of insight isn't free.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.450
So the question is,
what's this costing us?

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:25.660
Stackdriver Profiler works by
collecting data for 10 seconds

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.390
every one minute for
a single instance

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.200
of a configured service in a
single Google Compute Engine

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.220
Zone.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.330
For example, if you've got
10 GCE instances running,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.610
then the amortized overhead
of the CPU and heap allocation

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.110
profiling, at the time of data
collection, is less than 0.5%,

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.270
making it an affordable
option for always

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.541
on profiling in
production systems.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:51.040
If you're interested in getting
started with the Stackdriver

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.400
APM, check out the article
linked in the description

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:53.900
below.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.410
And don't forget to subscribe
to our YouTube channel

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.989
for more great "Cloud
Performance Atlas" content.

00:01:58.989 --> 00:02:00.530
Because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.080
every millisecond counts.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.130
